--TEST--
Core\Csv::parse answers the records a document holds — none for an empty one, none for a blank line, and none added by the terminator every generator writes — where str_getcsv answers exactly one record for every string including the empty one
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
`str_getcsv` has no notion of a record *count*: it answers one record for any string, so the empty document comes back as the one-field record `[null]` and a document of several records comes back as a single field-splitting of its first line — there is no PHP call that answers Novis's question, only the `explode("\n", …)` idiom around it, which counts a blank line as a record and the trailing terminator as one more.
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The field grammar inside one record is `str_getcsv`'s and the two languages
// agree on all of it — `csv-parse-reads-one-record-exactly-as-str_getcsv-does`
// is where that is pinned. What has no twin is the layer above it, which is the
// parsing half of `fgetcsv` rather than of `str_getcsv`: which byte runs ends a
// record, and which byte runs are not a record at all.
final class Show {
    public static function of(array<array<string>> $rows): string {
        var $out = "";
        foreach ($rows as array<string> $row) {
            $out = $out . "(";
            foreach ($row as string $field) {
                $out = $out . "[" . $field . "]";
            }
            $out = $out . ")";
        }
        return Core\Arr::count($rows) . $out;
    }
}

// The empty document holds no records. PHP answers `[null]` — one record of one
// null field — which is not a field value Novis's `array<array<string>>` can
// hold at all, so this is a type difference before it is a count difference.
echo Show::of(Core\Csv::parse("")), "\n";

// A blank line is not a record, and neither is a run of them. The idiom PHP
// reaches for here, `explode("\n", $doc)` mapped through `str_getcsv`, answers
// one `[null]` record per blank line.
echo Show::of(Core\Csv::parse("\n")), "\n";
echo Show::of(Core\Csv::parse("\r\n\r\n")), "\n";

// The terminator every generator writes after its last record does not add an
// empty one, and a document that omits it is the same document. Both spellings
// of the terminator are the same terminator.
echo Show::of(Core\Csv::parse("a,b\n")), "\n";
echo Show::of(Core\Csv::parse("a,b")), "\n";
echo Show::of(Core\Csv::parse("a,b\r\n")), "\n";

// Which puts the two rules together: a document of two records with a blank
// line between them and a terminator after the last holds two records, where
// the `explode` idiom counts four.
echo Show::of(Core\Csv::parse("a,b\n\nc,d\n")), "\n";

// A record whose only field is empty *is* a record, so the rule is not "skip a
// record with nothing in it" — it is that a line ending immediately is a
// terminator with no record before it. A single separator is the shortest
// document that shows the difference.
echo Show::of(Core\Csv::parse(",\n")), "\n";
--EXPECT--
0
0
0
1([a][b])
1([a][b])
1([a][b])
2([a][b])([c][d])
1([][])
